 "energy": "High-intensity intermittent: short sprints to lose a marker, sudden stops, jump-stops and shots, on an aerobic base (no korfball time-motion data, so netball and basketball are the model).",
 "summary": "Korfball is short sprints, hard stops, jump-stops and running-in shots, and ankle and knee injuries lead the injury list. The plan builds leg strength and jump power, with landing, deceleration and ankle balance work all year.",
 "evidence": [
  {
   "claim": "In 118 korfball players, the foot and ankle (34%) and knee (20%) were the most injured areas, and strains and sprains were the most common types.",
   "source": "Alp & Özdinç 2020, Turkish Journal of Sport and Exercise",
   "url": "https://doaj.org/article/5b505656ef1b49009ecbeb6377b0b29b"
  },
  {
   "claim": "In indoor korfball players aged 30 and over, injuries ran at 7.2 per 1,000 hours, 27% came from twisting and two thirds caused time out of sport.",
